      "answer": "The customer was BlueHarbor Logistics, where the issue began after the 2026-02-20 taxonomy rollout: saved searches started returning low-relevance hits and duplicates after that change. [art_bd3560dfe194]\n\nTo get them comfortable with renewal, we proposed a short-term proof-of-fix plan that included:\n- updating Event Nexus index weighting, and [art_bd3560dfe194]\n- adding a translation/mapping layer for the top 20 saved searches so new taxonomy terms map to historical query tokens. [art_bd3560dfe194]\n\nThe proposed proof plan timeline was:\n- 2026-03-19: BlueHarbor provides the schema registry export plus 14 days of query logs. [art_bd3560dfe194]\n- 2026-03-22: Jin and Isabella start an A/B test in staging. [art_bd3560dfe194]\n- run 5 business days to tune plus 2 business days validation. [art_bd3560dfe194]\n- if validation passes, do a 1-day production rollout followed by a 72-hour monitoring window. [art_bd3560dfe194]\n\nThe acceptance criteria for that proof were:\n- top-5 correct hit rate >= 80% for prioritized queries, [art_bd3560dfe194]\n- no regression in EN-RULES-ENGINE suppression logic, and [art_bd3560dfe194]\n- incident correlation latency <200ms. [art_bd3560dfe194]\n\nThe commercial wrapper around the renewal-comfort plan was a 90-day block at $48k fixed to do mapping, runbook docs, and handover, with a 30% discount if Orchestrator expansion signed concurrently. [art_bd3560dfe194]",
